WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:09.715
So where are we, Dan?

00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:10.590
Where are we heading?

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.423
North of the border.

00:00:11.423 --> 00:00:12.480
We're on the road again.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.110
We are going to
the Ke-new-ka Inn?

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:16.840
Kinneuchar Inn.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.640
How do you say it?

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.230
People who know
and are from here.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.616
it's pronounced Kinneuchar.

00:00:20.616 --> 00:00:21.116
Kinneuchar.

00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:21.616
Kinneuchar.

00:00:21.616 --> 00:00:23.010
Kinneuchar.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.620
It's an interesting place
because they use almost all

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.770
of the produce from the estate
in which the restaurant is

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.497
located.

00:00:29.497 --> 00:00:31.830
So some of the supply line
problems won't have hit them.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:32.280
That's good.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:32.988
That makes sense.

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:35.580
It's a real farm
to fork operation.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.300
They're aiming for
self-sufficiency,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.490
and I don't think
they're far away from it.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.900
TIM HAYWARD: Well, I'm
looking forward to it.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.942
I hope the weather holds.

00:00:51.265 --> 00:00:51.810
James, Hi.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:52.310
Hi.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:52.700
How are you doing.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.550
Nice to see you.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.403
Good to see you.

00:00:54.403 --> 00:00:55.320
It's good to meet you.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.375
Nice to meet you.

00:00:57.375 --> 00:00:58.625
So what do we got here, James?

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.980
So this is Bowhouse.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.020
We're on the Balcaskie Estate.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.390
And then just down
the way here is

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.960
where we have all our veg
growers, Tom and Connie.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.160
And then just literally
down the road,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.645
we've got some Monans and
Pittenweem, which is...

00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:15.510
That close?

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:16.010
That close.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:16.510
Yeah.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.950
Where we get all our
fish and shellfish.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.420
And then in here
is the butchery.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:21.920
OK.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:22.900
So you'll check it out?

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.410
So I'll leave the
butchery to you guys.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.730
Maybe I'll see you at the veg.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:26.870
I'm vegetarian of course.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:32.990
Wow.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:33.740
This is big.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.595
So what have we got here?

00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:40.220
This was just a bit
of four chords meat

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.345
that I've been chopping up.

00:01:41.345 --> 00:01:44.240
So getting some
stewing cuts out of it,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.170
getting some little
steak cuts out of it.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.710
So this is quite
an underused steak.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.960
Called the petite tender.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.260
But it's essentially sort
of second most tender

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.593
muscle next to a filet.

00:01:55.593 --> 00:01:56.510
No one knows about it.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.910
So it comes out right from
the middle of the shoulder.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.280
Normally that sort of just
comes off as a big piece

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.360
and it will just get chopped
up and put in trim or whatever.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.693
But if you just find this
little muscle and take it out.

00:02:05.693 --> 00:02:07.460
So I'm trying to get
as many steak cuts

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.645
as I can out of each cow.

00:02:09.645 --> 00:02:12.020
So using all these different
cuts which are traditionally

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.500
just thrown in with like
stewing or just into mince

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.780
and actually just trimming
them up to give people

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.235
more of a choice.

00:02:19.235 --> 00:02:20.720
I don't like to
work in a kitchen

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.860
where you're just buying in
like prime cuts, you know.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.090
And not just with cows.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.190
Like with sheep as
well it's kind of

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:32.180
odd to just buy like just a
solidly rack of lamb and stuff.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.930
It's more interesting
for me and much more

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.222
interesting for the restaurant.

00:02:35.222 --> 00:02:37.160
And you're doing sheep
and pigs here as well?

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:37.660
Yep.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.650
So sheep.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.540
The estate have
different breeds,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.970
which means they can actually
produce lamb all year round

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.788
because they sort of lamb
at slightly different times.

00:02:47.788 --> 00:02:49.830
And I'm also doing quite
a lot of mutton as well.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.940
So really getting the kind
of cull ewes that come from

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.550
the estate and-...

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.167
Tell me what a cull ewe is.

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:58.193
Cull ewe is just
a sheep that's not

00:02:58.193 --> 00:02:59.360
producing any lambs anymore.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.880
So it's done it's
kind of working life.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.513
I think you've also got
as butcher and as a chef,

00:03:05.513 --> 00:03:06.930
you do have a
responsibility I try

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.200
to educate people towards that.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.270
And we should be in
cull ewe not just

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.600
because I think it's ethical,
but because it's delicious.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.798
It's really delicious.

00:03:22.212 --> 00:03:23.420
So here we are on the estate.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.570
This is where you
get all the veg.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.028
This is where we get all the
veg, which is grown by Connie.

00:03:27.028 --> 00:03:28.662
How long have you
been here, Connie?

00:03:28.662 --> 00:03:32.150
We've been renting land
from the estate for three,

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.830
this is our third
growing season.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:35.750
It's a decent size
operation you've got.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:36.458
Wouldn't you say?

00:03:36.458 --> 00:03:38.750
It's Two acres,
three poly-tunnels.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.070
It's a no dig system.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.402
So you're getting 90-ish
percent of the vegetables you

00:03:42.402 --> 00:03:43.610
use in the kitchen from here?

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.150
Yeah.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.053
I mean we use one other
supplier which is over the way.

00:03:47.053 --> 00:03:48.470
And all the veg
that you would say

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.380
was the blockbuster veg that
can be the star of the dish all

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.340
comes from here.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.090
What difference does
that make when you're

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.200
getting stuff so quickly?

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.467
Sort of farm to fork.

00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:00.320
It's more important that we can
get really high quality veg.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.510
If we get something
that's very high quality

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.280
we don't want to mess
around with it too much.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.340
So we won't be adding
lots of butter and puree.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.290
We like to serve
it as it is so you

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.735
can taste how good it is and
hopefully cook it with respect.

00:04:13.735 --> 00:04:16.279
So we're hearing a lot about
the supply chain squeeze

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:17.089
at the moment.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.640
Does having access to products
like this on your doorstep

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:20.854
make you resilient to that?

00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:23.870
It makes us a lot more
resilient in terms

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.450
that we don't really have to
use like large scale suppliers

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.483
or as much fruit and veg as some
places might use from Europe.

00:04:29.483 --> 00:04:31.400
Are you one of the lucky
ones in that respect?

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.460
Not everybody has
access like this.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:33.960
Yeah.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.610
I think if you're going to have
a rural restaurant you should

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.405
be using sort of rural produce
as well that's grown out here.

00:04:41.405 --> 00:04:43.280
Because it seems something
crazy about having

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.560
a restaurant in Fife and then
going in sort of from London

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.090
to Edinburgh via Milan
in that sense to get veg.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.250
We work on a thing
where, like, they send me

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.530
an email on like
a Monday and then

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.690
they get the same with
Lewis who does the fish

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:01.920
and I know what there
is meat wise as well.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.430
So I'll see what's in
season and then that

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.590
will govern what the menu is.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.290
Rather than me say this
is a menu, I need this,

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:11.640
I'll always be like, what
do you have that's good?

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:12.140
Right.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.030
Let's have that, and
then let's decide

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.322
what we're going to do with it.

00:05:15.322 --> 00:05:18.870
And for me, that's just a more
fun, exciting way to cook.

00:05:19.202 --> 00:05:21.785
Does that hinder your chances
of ever getting a Michelin star?

00:05:21.785 --> 00:05:22.340
I mean...

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:23.090
I don't know.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:28.130
Look, I imagine there's
chefs that do work like that.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.910
A long time ago I decided
that I hadn't a big interest

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.575
in awards and stars.

00:05:33.575 --> 00:05:36.590
What I had an
interest in was people

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:41.570
sitting down and enjoying food,
and that gave me pleasure.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:43.230
This is all local
produce, right?

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.170
It's an amazing
selection of stuff.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.260
Oh, that is just a beauty.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:48.670
These are razor clams, right?

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:49.840
We call these spoose.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.340
Spoose.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:50.780
in Scotland.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.280
Yeah.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.072
You're a lucky man.

00:05:52.072 --> 00:05:52.572
Yeah.

00:05:52.572 --> 00:05:53.180
Yeah, I am.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:54.885
Because we've got
like the veg literally

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:56.510
like just down the
road, and then we've

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.220
got the meat same place.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.750
And then the fish here.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.322
And it's that freshness
that quality that hopefully

00:06:02.322 --> 00:06:03.530
makes its way onto the plate.

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.095
Were you involved in the
design of this place?

00:06:19.095 --> 00:06:22.520
So there was already a couple
who were the architects.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.660
Sort of coming on board
and doing the interiors.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.520
And then really when
we came on board

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.900
it was sort of nine months
before the end of the project.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.440
And so I then would
meet with the architects

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.500
because there was a sort
of whole idea of here,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.810
but also I just needed
to come in and get

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.330
a bit more practical.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:46.770
For example, the bar didn't
have that hole coming through.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:47.460
I had to fight--

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:48.030
Good Lord.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:48.880
Really?

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.880
...really hard to get
that because they said no,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.620
it doesn't really work
with whole visual.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.440
And I said, but this
is so necessary.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.970
Service flow is everything.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.230
And it's two extra members
of staff to cover the...

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.000
Yeah, so just little
things like that.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.325
But this was really built
from the inside out.

00:07:05.325 --> 00:07:08.100
You've ended up with an easy
utilitarianism aesthetic.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.055
Yeah there is sort of
sparsity, but also...

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:13.320
Yes, austere but comfortable.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.330
I wanted people to
feel that they're

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.270
being welcomed into our
home as it were, sort

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.910
of with the food and the
service and sort of feeling

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.143
comfortable from the
minute you arrive.

00:07:23.143 --> 00:07:24.435
What are you doing here, James?

00:07:24.435 --> 00:07:25.110
Yeah, sorry.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.240
So these are the
pork pies that we've

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.560
got at the moment
in the restaurant.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.360
So I'm just adding
the jelly, which

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.935
is traditional in a pork pie.

00:07:34.935 --> 00:07:37.980
I can't help but notice that
you're injecting this pie.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.945
Is that the
traditional technique?

00:07:39.945 --> 00:07:43.710
No I used to use a jug I've
found a little bit less messy

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.110
to use the sort
of meat injector.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.805
And also, quite
apt at the moment,

00:07:47.805 --> 00:07:49.607
like it's getting vaccinated.

00:07:49.607 --> 00:07:50.940
Is it going to be double jabbed?

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:51.990
Or triple jabbed.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.380
It's getting like 20
times jabbed, this one.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:58.545
Be completely immune from
anything by the time it's done.

00:07:58.545 --> 00:08:00.270
Tell me about your story, James.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.730
How long were you
working in London?

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.550
18 years working
in London kitchens?

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.020
So I grew up in a
restaurant which

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:09.240
was in Halifax, in Yorkshire.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.520
And then I moved to
London and this woman

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.950
Angela Hartnett was
opening at the Connaught

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.030
and they needed
chefs to be there.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.535
So I took the job there.

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:20.280
After 18 years in
London, what led you

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.765
to this corner of Scotland?

00:08:21.765 --> 00:08:24.090
Well basically I was asked
to look at this place.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.010
I bumped into someone
in Shoreditch.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.980
She was looking for a
chef to take over here,

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.480
and she offered for
me to come up...

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.429
they'd pay for us to
come up and have a look.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:33.840
I said yes and we came up.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.700
Then when we saw the
produce and everything

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.935
else that was around
there, we thought, well.

00:08:37.935 --> 00:08:41.070
Were you ever a bit concerned
it was sort of in a remote part

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:41.695
of the country?

00:08:41.695 --> 00:08:42.195
Yeah!

00:08:42.195 --> 00:08:44.190
I mean I was concerned
up to the point when...

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.490
before we opened
and when we opened.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.890
Anyone who opens a business,
especially somewhere

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:50.700
they don't know.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.575
Like an area they don't
know if they tell you

00:08:52.575 --> 00:08:55.680
that they're not concerned, then
I think they should have been.

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:06.260
This place from the outside,
is it's like a corner pub

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.480
in a very, very, very small...

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.090
I suppose village
is the word for it.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:10.917
Yeah.

00:09:10.917 --> 00:09:12.500
How were you greeted
when you arrived?

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:13.625
What happened with the locals?

00:09:13.625 --> 00:09:15.792
I think it was difficult,
because obviously this was

00:09:15.792 --> 00:09:18.080
a very old, traditional pub.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.980
Which previously everything
had been traditional fare,

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.050
as they refer to it.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.650
So it was quite
tricky to sort of open

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.820
and people suddenly had been
coming here for 20, 30 years

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:35.180
to suddenly come in and just
think, what is going on here?

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:38.810
We opened at the end
of September 2019

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.880
and then managed
to get six months.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.490
And we were working
incredibly hard

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:46.280
and we were really, really busy,
and then like everyone else

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.030
we just fell off a cliff.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.853
We then decided to do a
sort of voluntary service

00:09:51.853 --> 00:09:52.520
for the village.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.590
So that we made food at cost.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.960
I think people
really appreciated

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:58.520
that in the village,
and we suddenly

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.963
made quite a lot of friends.

00:10:00.963 --> 00:10:01.880
So what happened then?

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:05.195
You brought some guys up from
London to work in the kitchen.

00:10:05.195 --> 00:10:07.850
One of the guys that moved up
with his family and everything.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:10.550
And then after sort of
three months of the pandemic

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:12.288
he decided to move back down.

00:10:12.288 --> 00:10:13.955
And then the other
chef that was with me

00:10:13.955 --> 00:10:15.710
that had been with
me from the start,

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.740
he didn't want to
be around anymore.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.240
He wanted to be
down in Yorkshire

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.830
where he was originally from.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:23.570
Staffing hasn't really
sorted itself out.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.987
It's hard rurally
but I think it's

00:10:24.987 --> 00:10:26.960
been hard in big
cities like London

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.930
as well because I think some
people have had this time off

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:32.690
with furlough and they've
reassessed what they

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:33.940
want to do with their lives.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:36.755
It's been more of a
struggle to get staff.

00:10:36.755 --> 00:10:39.470
So it's one of the legacies of
the pandemic for the restaurant

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.210
business, do you
think, going to be

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.955
that staff are going to have
to be paid a little bit more?

00:10:43.955 --> 00:10:46.490
Well I think it's already
starting back in the day.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:49.040
Like when you started off
you got paid very little

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.290
and you worked a lot of hours.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.460
And it's like, well, you
just accepted it then.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.920
But I think these days people
just don't want to do that.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:56.840
And I can see why.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:57.845
Yeah.

00:10:57.845 --> 00:10:59.955
So you're opening up now
back up to full speed.

00:10:59.955 --> 00:11:00.455
Yeah.

00:11:00.455 --> 00:11:01.940
Are you finding staff?

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.990
Staffing is a problem.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:08.390
You know we are
struggling to find

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.000
another chef to come on board,
particularly where we are.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.730
Someone's got to make
sort of a life choice

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:16.820
to come and be here if they
want to work in a kitchen.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.010
It does feel
positive, but I think

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:21.840
it's about attracting people
to come and work for us.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.640
And therefore, we have to
manage expectations and also

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.700
look at how we run the
business and look at pricing

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:29.780
and all of those things.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.720
And pricing needs
to perhaps go up

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.140
and things need to
reflect people's wages,

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:37.880
people's way of life.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:40.340
It's trying to
find that balance.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.270
Great food.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.220
If you don't have
great service, it's

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:47.630
the two have to go hand in
hand because otherwise it

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:48.185
doesn't work.

00:11:56.555 --> 00:11:59.330
Tim, when James and Alethea
came up here in 2019

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.550
after sort of 18 years
working down in London,

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.200
it was already a bit of a punt.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.680
But then a few months later
along came Covid and completely

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:08.105
turn their plans upside down.

00:12:08.105 --> 00:12:10.580
It was definitely a risk,
but strangely mitigated.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.590
I mean they were
invited up here.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.010
They were recruited up here
by the Anstruther family who

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:18.500
own the estate up here,
and there was a plan,

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:22.160
I think, to try and create
a little bubble of food

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:23.378
appreciation and foodie-ness.

00:12:23.378 --> 00:12:24.920
It hasn't been plain
sailing, though.

00:12:24.965 --> 00:12:25.090
Oh, God no.

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:26.600
They had to win
hearts and minds.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.555
The locals didn't necessarily
take to them straight away.

00:12:29.555 --> 00:12:31.910
Extraordinarily difficult.
But what's happened is it's

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:33.390
a double-edged story.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:37.040
I think that they
were helped to set up.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:38.360
They got started.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:39.620
Covid hit.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.910
Covid immediately hit
them very, very hard.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:45.150
But also enabled them to forge
relationships with the people

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:45.650
around them.

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:48.410
Coming out of it, they've
been partially insulated

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:51.820
but they've also used that time
to become more flexible in what

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.820
they've thought about,
to think more creatively.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.220
And to establish themselves.

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:56.240
The food we're eating
here at the moment

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.657
is the kind of stuff you'd be
expected from a place that's

00:12:58.657 --> 00:12:59.450
been here 20 years.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:00.950
It's confident.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.460
It's well thought out, and
it mirrors the area around.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.210
Well, speaking of, the
squid on your plate

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:08.540
is the same squid that we
saw freshly caught yesterday.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:11.480
So they're really making the
most of this amazing produce

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.163
that's right on their doorstep.

00:13:13.163 --> 00:13:15.830
They're protected from all these
issues around squeeze of supply

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:16.887
chains that we're seeing.

00:13:16.887 --> 00:13:18.470
And they've got some
fantastic produce

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:21.632
which people might travel from
far and wide to come and see.

00:13:21.632 --> 00:13:23.090
It's a fascinating
all round story.

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:25.007
Different from other
Covid stories we've seen,

00:13:25.007 --> 00:13:26.750
but that's been the
most remarkable part

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:30.440
about going out and visiting
businesses during and after.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.360
It's not even just
a regional thing.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.670
It's not just that Scotland
has different Covid rules

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:35.470
to the UK.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:36.470
It's far more than that.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:37.700
It's a postcode.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.310
Literally another village
two kilometres off

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.560
that way will have a
totally different story.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.750
There really isn't a
difficulty getting people

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.790
to come here and eat great food.

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.010
What we're going to have
to worry about now is

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.590
are there enough people
to cook it, to serve it?

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.020
And will people pay enough
money to actually cover

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.140
the increasing costs of even
the lovely producers here.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:02.140
The price of that
is going to go up.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:04.250
Yes, still so much
uncertainty ahead.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.920
But as far as I'm concerned
from where I'm sitting,

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.935
I think this is
worth every penny.

00:14:09.935 --> 00:14:12.490
Yeah, I think you're right.

